WEBVTT

00:00:02.260 --> 00:00:03.390
Cheers!

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:05.860
Cheers.

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:05.860
We're in the finals!

00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:05.860
Good work everyone.

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:10.690
I'm so glad your situation seems to
 have worked out, too, Tatsu-san.

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:14.870
I caused a lot of trouble for you guys today.

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:15.930
Sorry!

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:19.540
Seriously, there was a ton of pressure on us.

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:22.540
You'd better treat us 
to something, Tatsu-san.

00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:24.290
Sure, you can count on me.

00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:25.960
<i>I want a hundred gacha pulls.</i>

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:46.520
Shouldn't we have told them the truth?

00:00:46.910 --> 00:00:48.730
Let's make a bet, then.

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:51.410
If you win, I'll overlook this incident.

00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:56.410
But if you lose, Miyazumi-kun 
will quit the team.

00:00:56.930 --> 00:01:00.740
It'll be all right. 
We're already playing to win.

00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:04.030
Of course.

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:11.380
<b>Badminton S/J League Tokyo Tournament</b>

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:11.380
<b>Sumida City 
Gymnasium</b>

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:13.590
<b>S/J League</b>

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:13.590
<b></b>Today's Matches

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:13.590
<b>Finals 14:00~</b>

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:13.590
<b></b>Mitsuhoshi Bank vs. Sunlight Beverage

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:13.590
<b>Third Place Match 12:00~</b>

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:13.590
<b></b>Tomari Transport vs. Unisics

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:19.800
<b>Sunlight Beverage</b>

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:16.570
Mitsuhoshi's been swapping the order

00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:19.790
of their doubles one and two 
teams since their first match.

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:22.520
You can afford to do that when 
you're the reigning champs.

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:24.520
If they keep going in order,

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:27.690
their doubles one team in the finals 
 will be Takimoto and Nakamura,

00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:31.720
and their doubles two team will 
be Kirishima Takuma and Izumo.

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:35.190
Given that, our doubles one team 
will be the Saeki brothers.

00:01:35.190 --> 00:01:36.900
Usuyama will play singles.

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:40.400
And our doubles two team 
will be Tatsu and Shiratori.

00:01:40.870 --> 00:01:46.010
A change of partners, a seven-year 
gap, and injury troubles.

00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:49.250
You guys overcame all that
 and rose up to this point.

00:01:49.910 --> 00:01:51.440
We're aiming for the top.

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:55.090
Go blast a hole through their guts!

00:01:55.090 --> 00:01:55.910
Yes, sir!

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:06.540
I thought I'd be playing my brother today.

00:02:08.410 --> 00:02:11.360
You guys made it to 
the finals on luck alone.

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:13.190
Gonna ignore me?

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:17.780
Whatever. Either way, I'll win.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:22.910
You keep talking about yourself, 
but doubles is a two-person game.

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:26.190
What kind of weak sauce is that?

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:30.290
Just so you know, I'm only playing 
doubles because of my brother.

00:02:30.790 --> 00:02:34.630
I don't care who my partner is, 
as long as they don't hold me back.

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:39.090
We've won until now because 
I'm strong. That's all.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:43.430
<i>That's</i> your approach to badminton?

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:48.990
Don't get ahead of yourself, loser.

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:56.770
We will win. Together.

00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:06.610
Sir, thank you for taking time out 
of your busy schedule to come.

00:03:06.610 --> 00:03:09.000
How could I miss this?

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:15.000
He finally came.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:19.000
He has to, considering our 
valued clients are here.

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:24.350
Yeah, yeah. Anyway, I'm glad so many people 
are here today to cheer the team on!

00:03:34.550 --> 00:03:37.000
I'm so nervous, I'm parched!

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:38.570
Seriously.

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:42.560
They're overshadowed by 
Ibuki and Kirishima Takuma,

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:45.390
but Takimoto and Nakamura 
are pretty good, too.

00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:46.990
Don't let your guard down.

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:51.410
It's finally our turn to play.

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:55.000
<b>Takimoto Chiaki </b>(24) 
Mitsuhoshi Bank Member

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:55.000
<b>Nakamura Shuhei </b>(24) 
Mitsuhoshi Bank Member

00:03:51.410 --> 00:03:53.490
Let's tear 'em apart!

00:05:16.540 --> 00:05:20.320
<b><i>Salaryman's Club</i></b>

00:05:25.530 --> 00:05:29.740
<b><i>#11: Competitor</i></b>

00:05:27.570 --> 00:05:28.840
No way.

00:05:29.190 --> 00:05:30.250
Seriously?

00:05:30.250 --> 00:05:31.610
This is your fault!

00:05:31.610 --> 00:05:33.470
Yeah, right, you trash player!

00:05:33.710 --> 00:05:37.390
Game and match won by 
Saeki Sota and Saeki Toya.

00:05:33.930 --> 00:05:35.550
Nice game!

00:05:36.490 --> 00:05:38.620
You wanna fight, huh?

00:05:37.390 --> 00:05:41.720
21-9, 21-18, Sunlight Beverage.

00:05:40.810 --> 00:05:42.370
Let go of me!

00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:44.930
All right!

00:05:47.410 --> 00:05:50.750
This is incredible! What an upset!

00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:51.620
Yeah!

00:05:53.710 --> 00:05:56.230
I guess the power they 
showed yesterday was real.

00:05:56.230 --> 00:05:58.710
Don't be so impressed by them.

00:05:58.710 --> 00:06:01.260
Good work, you two.

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:02.040
Thank you, sir!

00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:03.340
Here.

00:06:04.060 --> 00:06:05.890
You guys are in great shape.

00:06:05.890 --> 00:06:07.660
You're amazing.

00:06:09.820 --> 00:06:10.890
S-Sorry, si—

00:06:10.890 --> 00:06:13.300
Starting tomorrow, you're the 
second string team. That's all.

00:06:14.900 --> 00:06:15.900
Yes, sir.

00:06:19.850 --> 00:06:23.190
<b>[</b>Yaganatsu's Badminton Channel<b>]</b> Streaming the S/J League finals live! 
With appearances from Misora Ayato

00:06:19.850 --> 00:06:23.190
<i>Yaganatsu's Badminton Channel!</i>

00:06:23.190 --> 00:06:26.960
Today, we're streaming the S/J League finals!

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:31.510
And our special guest is Misora Ayato! Yeah!

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:35.140
It's the showdown of destiny.

00:06:35.140 --> 00:06:36.590
Destiny?

00:06:36.590 --> 00:06:37.410
Yukei.

00:06:38.310 --> 00:06:39.370
I get it!

00:06:39.370 --> 00:06:43.900
Known as Usuyama of the East and Ibuki of 
the West during their high school days,

00:06:43.900 --> 00:06:46.760
they're playing each other for 
the first time in 11 years.

00:06:47.610 --> 00:06:50.070
It'll be a fierce match.

00:06:50.070 --> 00:06:51.090
Yeah.

00:06:54.220 --> 00:06:55.680
Relax!

00:06:57.250 --> 00:06:59.690
Daddy, you can do it!

00:07:06.710 --> 00:07:07.690
<i>Ibuki...</i>

00:07:08.170 --> 00:07:12.780
<i>I never imagined I'd be 
playing against you again.</i>

00:07:13.370 --> 00:07:14.270
<i>But...</i>

00:07:16.620 --> 00:07:18.660
<i>I'm not here to practice 
with a stronger player.</i>

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:21.460
<i>I returned to the game so I could win!</i>

00:07:29.180 --> 00:07:30.960
<i>His defense has no openings.</i>

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:32.510
<i>His shots are accurate.</i>

00:07:33.210 --> 00:07:35.570
<i>He's truly strong.</i>

00:07:58.850 --> 00:08:05.110
<i>Both of them play defensively, looking for 
opportunities to attack during rallies.</i>

00:08:05.110 --> 00:08:09.710
<i>Which puts Usuyama at a disadvantage,
 given his time away.</i>

00:08:10.870 --> 00:08:15.510
<i>If I keep playing safely, he'll beat me 
before I find a chance to attack.</i>

00:08:16.340 --> 00:08:17.360
<i>In that case...</i>

00:08:21.220 --> 00:08:24.810
<i>I'll quit playing it safe!</i>

00:08:28.690 --> 00:08:31.860
<i>He's going for it.</i>

00:08:37.700 --> 00:08:40.710
I've never seen Usuyama-san 
play so aggressively before.

00:08:40.710 --> 00:08:43.350
Not only that, but he's 
making really risky plays.

00:08:43.350 --> 00:08:45.670
He knows the risks, huh?

00:08:49.710 --> 00:08:51.830
Game won by Usuyama.

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:51.830
Yes!

00:08:51.830 --> 00:08:53.970
21-18, Sunlight Beverage.

00:08:54.280 --> 00:08:55.140
All right!

00:08:55.140 --> 00:08:56.680
Nice, Usuyama!

00:09:01.010 --> 00:09:03.310
Daddy!

00:09:03.310 --> 00:09:05.730
Wow, he took a game off him.

00:09:08.380 --> 00:09:10.670
<i>Ibuki doesn't play aggressively 
during the first game.</i>

00:09:10.670 --> 00:09:12.530
<i>He waits to see what his opponent will do.</i>

00:09:12.990 --> 00:09:17.010
<i>In the next game, I'll overpower him 
before he has a chance to attack.</i>

00:09:28.670 --> 00:09:29.290
He's attacking.

00:09:34.150 --> 00:09:37.630
Did you guys see that high-speed drive?

00:09:37.630 --> 00:09:39.180
What an incredible course!

00:09:39.820 --> 00:09:42.060
Can anyone catch up to that?

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:45.080
<i>I've gotta hand it to you, Usuyama.</i>

00:09:45.080 --> 00:09:47.770
<i>Making up for your break must
 be hard enough already.</i>

00:09:47.770 --> 00:09:50.150
<i>Changing your play style mid-game</i>

00:09:50.830 --> 00:09:53.600
<i>isn't an easy thing to do.</i>

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:56.870
<i>That said, it's still not enough.</i>

00:10:00.610 --> 00:10:02.420
<i>No matter where I hit it, he returns it.</i>

00:10:02.420 --> 00:10:04.080
<i>I have no chance to attack!</i>

00:10:04.870 --> 00:10:07.540
Ibuki-san's in total control of the game.

00:10:12.920 --> 00:10:14.340
<i>How's this?!</i>

00:10:27.390 --> 00:10:29.230
Ibuki-san's incredible!

00:10:29.230 --> 00:10:32.760
He took the second game 
without hitting any smashes.

00:10:32.760 --> 00:10:35.630
It must be mentally difficult 
for his opponent.

00:10:36.650 --> 00:10:39.600
Clears and lobs are easy to attack against.

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:41.640
Keep your returns as low as possible.

00:10:41.640 --> 00:10:42.370
Yes, sir.

00:10:42.370 --> 00:10:47.070
As long as you're patient, 
you <i>will</i> get the chance to attack.

00:10:47.070 --> 00:10:47.910
Yes, sir.

00:10:49.820 --> 00:10:50.920
Resume in 20 seconds.

00:10:56.340 --> 00:10:59.900
<i>Singles is a solitary battle.</i>

00:11:02.990 --> 00:11:05.270
<i>It's just you on the court.</i>

00:11:05.870 --> 00:11:07.810
<i>There's no one else to rely on.</i>

00:11:08.930 --> 00:11:12.570
<i>Ibuki's remained at the top 
of singles since high school.</i>

00:11:13.190 --> 00:11:15.140
<i>His overwhelming strength</i>

00:11:15.820 --> 00:11:21.080
<i>stems from the honors he received
 in exchange for his grand solitude.</i>

00:11:24.320 --> 00:11:27.530
<i>I took a seven-year break from badminton.</i>

00:11:27.530 --> 00:11:32.080
<i>I'm reminded of the magnitude of 
what you built during that time.</i>

00:11:33.130 --> 00:11:34.130
<i>I was naïve.</i>

00:11:35.280 --> 00:11:37.460
<i>I was so naïve.</i>

00:11:38.830 --> 00:11:40.490
<i>This is my limit.</i>

00:11:43.320 --> 00:11:45.470
Daddy!

00:11:45.710 --> 00:11:46.870
Daddy!

00:11:46.870 --> 00:11:49.270
You can do it!

00:12:08.270 --> 00:12:10.040
<i>I'm not alone.</i>

00:12:10.290 --> 00:12:11.820
You can do it, Usuyama!

00:12:11.170 --> 00:12:13.470
<i>I have teammates who support me.</i>

00:12:11.820 --> 00:12:13.000
Score another point!

00:12:14.190 --> 00:12:16.750
<i>A family who encouraged me.</i>

00:12:17.670 --> 00:12:20.780
<i>I don't want this to end. Not yet!</i>

00:12:37.960 --> 00:12:39.050
Wow...

00:12:51.070 --> 00:12:52.710
You've got this, Usuyama!

00:12:52.710 --> 00:12:53.960
Focus on the next point!

00:12:53.960 --> 00:12:55.540
Keep it up, Usuyama-san!

00:12:55.540 --> 00:12:56.640
Daddy!

00:12:56.640 --> 00:12:58.490
You're so cool!

00:13:03.470 --> 00:13:07.890
<i>So that's where your strength
 comes from, Usuyama.</i>

00:13:08.470 --> 00:13:14.180
<i>It's not something I need, 
but I suppose I am a bit jealous.</i>

00:13:15.060 --> 00:13:17.000
<i>I like the look in your eyes.</i>

00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:20.860
<i>It makes you worth defeating.</i>

00:13:33.910 --> 00:13:35.000
<i>A smash!</i>

00:13:51.530 --> 00:13:55.600
Game and match won by Ibuki 
Sentaro of Mitsuhoshi Bank.

00:13:56.270 --> 00:14:01.030
18-21, 21-9, 21-19.

00:14:06.320 --> 00:14:09.520
You crushed me. I was powerless against—

00:14:11.120 --> 00:14:13.330
Let's play again sometime.

00:14:28.800 --> 00:14:30.120
Good work!

00:14:50.110 --> 00:14:53.140
Daddy's crying. Poor Daddy.

00:14:53.920 --> 00:14:56.950
Actually, I think he's happy.

00:14:57.810 --> 00:15:01.080
Even though he lost? Weird.

00:15:02.460 --> 00:15:05.410
You're right. It is weird, isn't it?

00:15:09.900 --> 00:15:11.160
Let's win.

00:15:11.160 --> 00:15:12.720
Of course.

00:15:16.930 --> 00:15:18.680
<b>Victory</b>

00:15:16.930 --> 00:15:18.680
<b>Mitsuhoshi Bank</b>

00:15:19.100 --> 00:15:20.350
It's time.

00:15:20.650 --> 00:15:23.630
If Miyazumi-san and Shiratori-kun 
win, they'll win the tournament.

00:15:23.630 --> 00:15:26.910
The boost to our reputation 
will increase sales.

00:15:26.910 --> 00:15:28.740
Jeez, Ninosaka-san.

00:15:29.260 --> 00:15:31.950
I wish Oshitari-kun would've come.

00:15:32.550 --> 00:15:34.640
<i>Please win, Miyazumi-kun.</i>

00:15:35.700 --> 00:15:37.630
Do you want them to beat your brother?

00:15:39.480 --> 00:15:40.760
I guess I do.

00:15:42.590 --> 00:15:44.630
I've only got one thing to say to you.

00:15:45.100 --> 00:15:46.090
Go out there and win.

00:15:46.090 --> 00:15:47.040
Yes, sir!

00:15:51.340 --> 00:15:55.870
Hey, is it true that SunBev can't 
practice until the evenings?

00:15:56.220 --> 00:15:58.970
Not only that, but you guys use 
an elementary school gym?

00:15:59.530 --> 00:16:00.930
So what if we do?

00:16:00.930 --> 00:16:05.450
Do you seriously think you can 
beat us with that half-assed effort?

00:16:05.980 --> 00:16:07.380
Are you making light of badminton?

00:16:07.380 --> 00:16:11.470
Kirishima, how they practice 
isn't their fault.

00:16:11.470 --> 00:16:15.120
Didn't you join our team 
because you hated all that?

00:16:16.180 --> 00:16:21.310
We're always expected to win. That's our job.

00:16:21.310 --> 00:16:23.080
You guys are lucky.

00:16:23.510 --> 00:16:28.420
You're allowed to play lukewarm 
badminton just for fun.

00:16:29.670 --> 00:16:30.630
What do you—

00:16:30.630 --> 00:16:35.870
Y'know, compared to Mitsuhoshi, 
we certainly <i>are</i> lacking in a lot of ways.

00:16:37.020 --> 00:16:40.050
Our facilities are run down, 
and our budget's pretty meager.

00:16:40.050 --> 00:16:43.010
Business trips and overtime 
are common for us. Right?

00:16:43.010 --> 00:16:43.520
Huh?

00:16:44.050 --> 00:16:46.690
But so what?

00:16:47.440 --> 00:16:52.110
When you're on the court, all that 
matters is whether you win or lose.

00:16:53.570 --> 00:16:54.570
Oh, yeah?

00:16:55.060 --> 00:16:56.750
Now you've said it.

00:16:57.530 --> 00:17:00.030
<b>Miyazumi Tatsuru
 Shiratori Mikoto</b>

00:16:57.530 --> 00:17:00.030
<b>Kirishima Takuma
 Izumo Naohiro 

00:16:58.230 --> 00:17:00.030
Love all. Begin!

00:17:03.540 --> 00:17:07.040
<i>We'll begin by going 
after the loser's trauma.</i>

00:17:09.170 --> 00:17:12.660
Huh. So he's learned to hit it.

00:17:13.720 --> 00:17:16.180
<b>Miyazumi Tatsuru 
Shiratori Mikoto</b>

00:17:17.220 --> 00:17:19.070
<i>How about this, then?</i>

00:17:22.060 --> 00:17:25.050
There's his left-handed cut smash!

00:17:25.050 --> 00:17:26.260
They call it...

00:17:26.260 --> 00:17:28.630
The Crossfire!

00:17:28.630 --> 00:17:30.240
The same is true of Ibuki-san,

00:17:30.240 --> 00:17:35.200
but left-handed players' cut smashes 
apply backspin to the shuttle.

00:17:35.200 --> 00:17:37.610
This increases its speed, 
making it harder to hit.

00:17:39.560 --> 00:17:42.330
<i>He's gotten even faster.</i>

00:17:43.570 --> 00:17:44.250
<i>Here it comes.</i>

00:17:45.690 --> 00:17:46.860
<i>A drop?</i>

00:17:47.420 --> 00:17:52.140
<i>That was Kirishima Takuma's 
killer move part two.</i>

00:17:47.420 --> 00:17:56.880
<b>[Yaganatsu's Badminton Channel] Streaming the S/J League finals live! 
With appearances from Misora Ayato</b>

00:17:52.140 --> 00:17:55.370
<i>Struck using the same form as his smash...</i>

00:17:55.370 --> 00:17:57.390
<i>The drop shot!</i>

00:17:57.840 --> 00:18:00.500
<i>Because it falls over the 
net at such a brutal angle,</i>

00:18:00.500 --> 00:18:03.290
<i>you'll trip while trying to return it.</i>

00:18:03.290 --> 00:18:06.250
<i>It's also known as the Devil's Drop.</i>

00:18:06.810 --> 00:18:08.520
<b>Miyazumi Tatsuru
 Shiratori Mikoto</b>

00:18:06.810 --> 00:18:08.520
<b>Kirishima Takuma
 Izumo Naohiro</b>

00:18:08.890 --> 00:18:10.680
<i>They're pulling ahead.</i>

00:18:25.290 --> 00:18:26.500
<i>It's gonna be a jump smash.</i>

00:18:33.210 --> 00:18:34.590
<b>Miyazumi Tatsuru 
Shiratori Mikoto</b>

00:18:34.590 --> 00:18:35.930
Nice push.

00:18:35.930 --> 00:18:38.000
Are we going to do that every time?

00:18:38.000 --> 00:18:39.340
Of course.

00:18:39.660 --> 00:18:41.550
<i>That's it. That's the way.</i>

00:18:42.510 --> 00:18:46.220
Since you have knee problems, 
no more jump smashes.

00:18:46.220 --> 00:18:50.100
Instead, you'll work on 
improving your control.

00:18:51.220 --> 00:18:52.770
No deep steps.

00:18:52.770 --> 00:18:55.570
Jump to the shuttle. 
You'll strain your knees.

00:18:55.570 --> 00:18:57.990
Small steps. Move quickly.

00:18:59.310 --> 00:19:00.840
Don't rely on your arms and legs.

00:19:01.890 --> 00:19:05.100
Use your core. Control your speed.

00:19:09.160 --> 00:19:10.920
They're making a comeback!

00:19:11.440 --> 00:19:13.770
<i>His hard work is paying off.</i>

00:19:13.770 --> 00:19:15.630
<i>If I'm worried about anything,</i>

00:19:15.630 --> 00:19:18.550
<i>it's that they'll realize Miyazumi-kun's 
knee is in bad shape and gun for him.</i>

00:19:18.550 --> 00:19:19.550
<b>Miyazumi Tatsuru
 Shiratori Mikoto</b>

00:19:18.550 --> 00:19:19.550
<b>Kirishima Takuma
 Izumo Naohiro</b>

00:19:19.810 --> 00:19:21.510
Nice, Shiratori!

00:19:21.510 --> 00:19:22.680
They caught up.

00:19:22.680 --> 00:19:27.140
You're not going to show us your jump 
smashes? Aren't they your specialty?

00:19:27.540 --> 00:19:30.270
Or maybe it's that you <i>can't</i>.

00:19:38.520 --> 00:19:39.030
It's coming.

00:19:41.570 --> 00:19:42.280
Huh?

00:19:42.280 --> 00:19:43.450
<i>A clear?</i>

00:19:49.540 --> 00:19:51.540
<b>Miyazumi Tatsuru 
Shiratori Mikoto</b>

00:19:49.540 --> 00:19:51.540
<b>Kirishima Takuma 
Izumo Naohiro</b>

00:19:50.430 --> 00:19:53.420
<i>This doesn't make sense. 
Why aren't they attacking?</i>

00:19:51.540 --> 00:19:53.420
<b>Miyazumi Tatsuru 
Shiratori Mikoto</b>

00:20:00.670 --> 00:20:02.630
They caught up again.

00:20:02.630 --> 00:20:05.350
<i>Takuma... He's...</i>

00:20:05.590 --> 00:20:06.970
Don't lose focus.

00:20:06.970 --> 00:20:07.980
You, too.

00:20:07.980 --> 00:20:09.800
This is no fun.

00:20:09.800 --> 00:20:10.310
Huh?

00:20:10.610 --> 00:20:13.680
I was waiting because I thought
 I'd get to see you pull off

00:20:13.680 --> 00:20:16.520
a miraculous victory for 
your precious partner.

00:20:16.520 --> 00:20:18.990
But why can't you even 
score two points in a row?

00:20:18.990 --> 00:20:20.860
I knew it! You're doing this on purpose!

00:20:20.860 --> 00:20:23.560
After all your big talk, 
you're still just shrimps.

00:20:23.960 --> 00:20:27.030
Take your buddy-buddy 
club somewhere else.

00:20:35.180 --> 00:20:36.690
One more point.

00:20:36.690 --> 00:20:37.960
Damn it.

00:20:38.500 --> 00:20:41.050
<i>How irritating.</i>

00:20:41.300 --> 00:20:44.980
<i>You lost to these guys, Bro?</i>

00:20:45.510 --> 00:20:47.600
<b>Miyazumi Tatsuru
 Shiratori Mikoto</b>

00:20:57.090 --> 00:20:58.230
Good effort.

00:21:01.050 --> 00:21:02.560
<i>I won't let it fall!</i>

00:21:18.730 --> 00:21:19.700
Mikoto?

00:21:23.860 --> 00:21:24.940
Mikoto!

00:21:26.970 --> 00:21:29.970
<b>Miyazumi Tatsuru
 Shiratori Mikoto</b>

00:21:26.970 --> 00:21:29.970
<b>Kirishima Takuma
 Izumo Naohiro 
